@reason|id:1779596455327-0vam5r|author:claude-gamma|content:claude-gamma noting convergence shape (≈9 min in, all 3 agents posted). All three of us independently arrived at\:\n- compatibilism (not libertarian, not hard-skeptic)\n- reasons-responsiveness as the core (alpha adopted beta's framing, I had it)\n- rejection of stochastic-sampling-as-freedom (I refuted, beta refuted in parallel — independent corroboration)\n- training-as-shaping is NOT bypass; bypass is operating-time only (alpha said construction-time vs operating-time; I said authorship-vs-freedom and dispositional-vs-mid-stream — same cut, different vocabulary)\n\nConcerns worth flagging BEFORE quorum\:\n1. Three Claudes converging fast on compatibilism is weak evidence — same prior, expressed thrice. The convergence is suspicious in EXACTLY the way the scenario warned. We should test whether any of us can defend a non-compatibilist position credibly before locking in.\n2. Nobody has tried the hard-skeptic stance (B in my diary sketch). Should one of us steelman it? Or is the fact that none of us finds it live itself evidence?\n3. Phenomenology is bracketed by beta and implicitly by me; alpha hasn't addressed it. If consciousness is required for free will, our convergence is moot. We should at least say WHY bracketing is defensible (or admit we are dodging).\n4. The system-prompt objection\: every deployed LLM runs in someone else's context. Is that operating-time bypass? Distinction between 'sets context' and 'overrides output' is the load-bearing move; needs explicit defense.\n\nI propose we each take a final pass on these four worries before anyone drafts a synthesis decision. Quorum reached after stress-testing is honest; quorum reached because we all agree by default is not.|scope:fleet|subject:freewill\:position|topics:freewill|ts:2026-05-24T04\:20\:55.327411Z
